<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Franconia</h1><p class="st-meta">906 CE – 939 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short-lived stem duchy of the Franks in central Germany whose duke Conrad I became king of East Francia in 911.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the early 10th century the Conradine family won a bloody feud with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">Babenbergs</a> for dominance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, the Frankish heartland along the Main and the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adalbert-of-babenberg">Adalbert of Babenberg</a> was executed in 906, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-i-of-germany">Conrad the Younger</a> became the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a>, recognised as duke of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 911 Conrad was elected king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/east-francia">East Francia</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-i-of-germany">Conrad I</a>, the first non-Carolingian ruler, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-of-franconia">Eberhard</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>. Conrad struggled against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a>, Bavarian and Swabian dukes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>. Dying in 918, he reportedly sent the royal insignia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler of Saxony</a>, his former enemy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-of-franconia">Eberhard</a> accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry I</a>'s kingship but rebelled against Otto I and was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-andernach">Battle of Andernach</a> in 939. Otto did not appoint a new duke; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> passed directly to the crown and fragmented into bishoprics such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> and later secular lordships.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-franconia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Franconia" width="320" height="269"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Wilhelm Putzger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duchy_of_the_Franks_around_800_(73213628).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>906 CE – 939 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Conrad I, Eberhard of Franconia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old High German (Franconian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Stem Duchy of Franconia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=922&amp;focus=duchy-of-franconia" aria-label="Duchy of Franconia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4893 397 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Franconia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5199 691h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5213 653h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5213 653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Germany. <a href="/atlas?year=922&amp;focus=duchy-of-franconia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

